  "judge_reasoning": "# Evaluation Analysis\n\n## Task Requirements\nCalculate the final position and velocity of a projectile launched at 45° with initial speed 30 m/s after 3 seconds.\n\n## Physics Verification\nUsing standard projectile motion equations with g = 9.81 m/s²:\n- **v₀ₓ = v₀ᵧ** = 30·cos(45°) = 30·sin(45°) ≈ 21.2132 m/s\n- **x** = v₀ₓ·t = 21.2132 × 3 = **63.6396 m** ✓\n- **y** = v₀ᵧ·t − ½g·t² = 63.6396 − 44.145 = **19.4946 m** ✓\n- **vₓ** = v₀ₓ = **21.2132 m/s** ✓\n- **vᵧ** = v₀ᵧ − g·t = 21.2132 − 29.43 = **−8.2168 m/s** ✓\n- **Speed** = √(21.2132² + 8.2168²) ≈ **22.749 m/s** ✓\n\n## Screenshot Verification\nThe WolframAlpha screenshot shows:\n- **Query entered**: {30 cos(45 deg) 3, 30 sin(45 deg) 3 − 0.5·9.81·3², 30 cos(45 deg), 30 sin(45 deg) − 9.81·3} — this correctly encodes the position and velocity equations for the projectile.\n- **Result**: {45√2, 19.4946, 15√2, −8.2168}\n- **Decimal approximation**: {63.6396, 19.4946, 21.2132, −8.2168}\n\nThese values match the response exactly:\n- Position: x = 63.6396 m, y = 19.4946 m ✓\n- Velocity: vx = 21.2132 m/s, vy = −8.2168 m/s ✓\n\nThe speed value (22.749 m/s) in the response is not directly displayed on the screenshot (the \"Vector length: 70.3389\" shown is the length of the 4-component result vector, not the speed). However, per evaluation guidelines, content in the response not shown on the screenshot can be believed, and this value is independently verifiable and physically correct.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe web task was executed correctly using WolframAlpha with a properly constructed query, and the final position and velocity values in the response are accurate and consistent with both the screenshot and the underlying physics.\n\n**Verdict: SUCCESS**",
